WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
Injection review starts by identifying trust boundaries.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:21.160
In ASP.NET and ASP.NET Core applications, untrusted data can enter through request bodies, query strings, route parameters, headers, cookies, JSON payloads, XML payloads, form fields, multipart uploads, and SignalR or API messages.

3
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:35.900
Backend services add more sources: message queues, webhooks, third-party API responses, imports, environment variables, configuration values, command-line arguments, and data loaded from storage.

4
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.020
A common mistake is treating internal-looking data as automatically safe.

5
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:50.680
A queue message may have come from another team, a configuration value may be changed during deployment, and a third-party API response may reflect attacker-controlled input from somewhere else.

6
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:59.660
If a value can cross a boundary before your code uses it, the value should be treated as untrusted until the application constrains it for the specific purpose at hand.

7
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.140
Data-flow mapping connects sources to sensitive use.

8
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:11.560
A query parameter might become a DTO property, then a service option, then a repository filter, then part of a raw SQL statement.

9
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:19.820
A file upload name might pass through validation, logging, storage selection, and an external process adapter.

10
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.720
A JSON field might become an expression, a URL, or an outbound request parameter.

11
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.660
The security review needs to follow the value all the way to the sink.

12
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.220
Good C# design makes these transitions visible.

13
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.680
Convert raw strings into typed values early.

14
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:43.780
Use enums or known option objects for choices such as sort direction, export type, report format, and search field.

15
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.200
Separate external request models from internal domain models.

16
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:54.060
Keep validation close to the boundary, but also verify that the final API treats the data safely.

17
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.680
Clear data flow makes injection risks easier to find, test, and explain during review.
